  stdout capture.

  [1mClassification: bounded.[0m [38;5;153msummarize[39m already renders and emits a summary; [38;5;153mexport[39m writes the same thing to a file
  instead. So I'll ask a few questions, present a short design in chat, and stop for your approval — no spec file, no
  plan document. If the format question turns this into a pluggable-renderer subsystem, I'll say so and upgrade.

  First question, and it's the one that actually shapes the code:

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether [38;5;153mexport[39m is purely a [3mdestination[0m change (same bytes as [38;5;153msummarize[39m, just landing in a file) or also a
  [3mformat[0m change (a machine-readable rendering that [38;5;153msummarize.py[39m doesn't have yet).

  - [1mPlain text only[0m — reuse [38;5;153mrender_text()[39m as-is, zero new rendering code, one small file-write path. Costs nothing now;
    if you later want JSON you add it then, and adding a [38;5;153m--format[39m flag to an existing [38;5;153mexport[39m is cheap.
  - [1mJSON only[0m — implies the consumer is a program, not a person. Needs a new renderer and a decision about
    cents-vs-dollars in the payload. Ruling out text means the exported file is no longer the thing ops already reads in
    email.
  - [1mBoth, via [38;5;153m--format[0m — most flexible, but it's two renderers plus flag plumbing up front, and [38;5;153m--format[39m is the piece
    that's hard to remove later once someone scripts against it.

  [1mMy recommendation: plain text only.[0m The file's job right now is to be the same artifact the cron mail already carries,
  just durable on disk. Adding formats is a cheap follow-up; picking a format nobody consumes yet is speculative work,
  and [38;5;153m--format[39m is the one choice here that's expensive to walk back.
